<% content_for :title, "Accessibility — Shadwire" %>
<% content_for :description, "What Shadwire components guarantee about accessibility, what stays your responsibility, and how the project's suite checks it." %>
<% content_for :head do %>
  <%= tag.style rouge_stylesheet %>
<% end %>

<article class="space-y-12">
  <%= render "docs/header",
        title: "Accessibility",
        description: "The components follow the WAI-ARIA APG patterns and prefer the native element wherever one exists. What is left for you is short." %>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("What comes built in") %>

    <%= render "docs/table",
          headers: [ "Area", "What the component already does" ],
          rows: [
            [ "Native element first", safe_join([ "Dialog, Alert Dialog, Sheet and Drawer use the native ", docs_code("<dialog>"), ": focus trapping, ", docs_code("Esc"), " and focus restoration come from the browser. Checkbox, Radio Group and Switch are styled native inputs." ]) ],
            [ "Roles and states", safe_join([ "Tabs exposes ", docs_code("tablist"), "/", docs_code("tab"), "/", docs_code("tabpanel"), "; Select is an APG combobox with ", docs_code("aria-activedescendant"), "; Toggle uses ", docs_code("aria-pressed"), "; Progress exposes ", docs_code("aria-valuenow"), "." ]) ],
            [ "Keyboard", "Arrow keys navigate menus, tabs, select and toggle group; typeahead matches by prefix in menus and select; Esc closes overlays." ],
            [ "Visible focus", safe_join([ "Every interactive control carries the focus ring from the ", docs_code("ring"), " token, including the ", docs_code(":focus-visible"), " state." ]) ],
            [ "Decoration is hidden", safe_join([ "Icons render ", docs_code('aria-hidden="true"'), " by default; decorative separators leave the accessibility tree." ]) ]
          ] %>

    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Every component has render tests in the repository, and an accessibility suite walks the
      site's pages checking structure, labelling and navigation. The system tests run in headless
      Chrome on every change.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("What stays yours") %>

    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Naming overlays") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          <%= docs_code("<dialog>") %> gives you focus and keyboard handling, but it does not
          invent a name. Every dialog, sheet and drawer needs its title component; use
          <%= docs_code('class: "sr-only"') %> when the design shows none.
        </p>
        <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:overlay]) %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Naming icons that carry meaning") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          An icon beside text is decoration and should stay hidden. An icon on its own becomes
          the label, so pass <%= docs_code("label:") %> or label the control that contains it.
          Details under <%= link_to "Icons", docs_icons_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
        </p>
        <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:icon_label]) %>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Tying label to control") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          Since there is no form builder integration, the
          <%= docs_code("for:") %> / <%= docs_code("id:") %> pair is yours.
          <%= docs_code("ui_field") %> lays the row out, but it does not guess the control's id.
          See <%= link_to "Forms", docs_forms_path, class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Contrast after retheming") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          The default values pass the contrast criteria; yours have to be checked. Always set the
          <%= docs_code("<surface>") %> / <%= docs_code("<surface>-foreground") %> pair together,
          and test in both modes.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= docs_h3("Heading hierarchy") %>
        <p class="text-sm leading-6 text-muted-foreground">
          <%= docs_code("ui_card_title") %> and <%= docs_code("ui_dialog_title") %> are style, not
          level. If the page needs a heading structure, write the
          <%= docs_code("h1") %>–<%= docs_code("h3") %> around them yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Testing in your app") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      The installed components are your code, so they test like any ViewComponent. Pin the
      accessible name in a test — that is the part the library cannot guarantee on its own:
    </p>

    <%= docs_code_block(@snippets[:test], language: :ruby) %>

    <%= render layout: "docs/callout", locals: { icon: "info", title: "Interactive components need a browser" } do %>
      Focus, keyboard handling and <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> behaviour only show up in a system
      test. A render test confirms the markup, not that <code>Esc</code> closes anything.
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="space-y-4">
    <%= docs_h2("Found a problem?") %>
    <p class="leading-7 text-muted-foreground">
      Broken accessibility in a component is a registry bug, not a bug in your app:
      <%= link_to "open an issue", "https://github.com/edumoraes/shadwire/issues/new", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", class: "font-medium underline underline-offset-4" %>.
      In the meantime the file is yours, so fix it locally and run
      <%= docs_code("bin/shadwire diff") %> before your next update.
    </p>
  </section>
</article>
